Hamish Marson (hamish@thebes.cc.waikato.ac.nz) wrote:

: Umm.. Has anyone noticed anything odd with osf/1's mail? Like re-writing
: the first word of each line to be uppercase? Or am I just (un)lucky? Is
: this an undocumented feature?

: 4 instance... I mail myself the password file with

: mail user@wherever < /etc/passwd

: and the file comes out like this

: Root: Hjdsflkjmn:0...
: ^^^^ ^^

: Notice the capitalisation, and the space inserted before the password...

	I think you'll find it wouldn't capitalise it if you did this:

(echo "" ; cat /etc/passwd) | mail user@wherever

	Why? Well, until it sees either a blank line, or something to
convince it it's not in header land, it assumes it's all headers. The
password file is full of "Valid-ish-type-header-name:lots-of-data", so
mail thinks the entire file is a ton of headers and acts accordingly.

	You get a similar fun effect (I first noticed this from our
News system on Ultrix) from cron jobs where all output is of the form
"commandname: message string", where most of the output ends up in
the headers. :-)

Hope this helps,
		Andrew.

