Summary: Is this the end for DME?  Who is responsible?
Keywords: DME, standards, network management, systems management


The following is a draft of an article that will appear in the March issue
of the Network and Systems Managers' Best Practices Report.  I realize
that the viewpoint expressed here will not be popular with many.  However,
in replying, please don't just argue--as many have--that "DME will succeed
because it is very important for DME to succeed." Also, the newsletter's
subscribers are mostly upper-level IS management and CIOs, so the
responses that will be most useful will have their concerns in mind,
focusing on larger issues rather than technical minutia.


			*	*	* 


			Who _Really_ Killed DME?


	Ever since word got out that BPR was planning an article on the
death of the Distributed Management Environment (DME), we've been
receiving a stream of email from vendors and managers trying to convince
us that reports of DME's death are premature.  Most of these letters have
stressed how vitally important it is for the future of systems and network
management that the effort not be dead. 

	We sympathize with these concerned citizens of the IS management
world.  Indeed, BPR would like nothing better than to announce widespread
support for DME among hardware vendors, ISVs and users alike.  Imagine a
day when users would benefit from truly "open standards" providing
complete portability of network and systems management tools across
hardware and operating system platforms.  Users would be able to choose
best-of-breed products from a multi-vendor library of "plug and play"
management software components--without having to worry about
inter-operability.  The user would finally be in the driver's seat. 

	Alas, it's not going to happen.  Whatever is salvaged from the
Open Software Foundation's four- year, multi-million dollar DME effort
will offer only a faint shadow of the benefits once promised.  Not only
are IS managers not demanding a commitment to DME compliance when
considering purchases, but most have never even heard of the standard. 
Facing an immediate need for management functionality, users are turning
to proprietary software solutions from companies like HP, IBM, Computer
Associates, and Tivoli.  And, despite vague assurances from some vendors
that they will move to support DME in the future, consensus in the
industry is clearly that DME, if and when it arrives, will be too little,
too late. 

	Although the failure of DME is unfortunate, it is not exactly news
to the few managers who have been keeping up with the OSF's progress.  For
the most part, they lay the blame for DME's demise on the delays in
development which have plagued the effort--most recently, a major change
in the method of handling object requests which has required significant
re-engineering of the technology.  Moreover, it will be another year or
two after release before OSF members will be able to convert the DME
technology into compliant commercial products.  In the interim, most IS
managers will have chosen to implement proprietary solutions. 

	Technical delays are certainly the most obvious reason for DME's
failure.  However, the root causes lie elsewhere.  DME did not die of old
age--rather, was a victim of vendor greed and user neglect.  And its death
has clear implications for other important standards efforts in the UNIX
world, such as the COSE initiative to standardize the UNIX operating
system. 

				*	*	*

	OSF was founded on the assumption that hardware vendors would
willingly join together to create "open standards" which would ensure
portability and inter-operability of all management software.  As a
result, users would enjoy freedom of choice and lower costs through
competition in plug-and-play components. 

	But what would happen if the promise of truly open standards were
ever attained?  The market for management products would be reduced to
commodity pricing.  Hardware and software would be so simple to switch
that price would become the primary criterion in user purchasing
decisions. 

	Is it really sensible to believe that vendors would support an
effort to drive them into a commodity marketplace, complete with low
margins, reduced R&D expenditures, and low-cost competition from Asia
and--before long--Eastern Europe?  In a rare moment of honesty, one of
Digital Equipment Corporation's senior officials told 3,500 Digital users
that "OSF takes the profit out of innovation." (A short time later,
Digital announced $400 million in losses.  That executive left Digital at
about the same time.)

	A better explanation is that these vendors paid tens of millions
of dollars supporting OSF in order to stop their competitors--in
particular Sun and AT&T--from gaining a dominant market share through
their own effort at standardization.  Basic survival instinct, not service
to users, motivated the formation of OSF. 

				*	*	*

	In this reading of events, the challenge for OSF's sponsors became
one of projecting a public appearance of favoring open standards for
management while simultaneously building or gathering a proprietary set of
tools to perform exactly the same functions.  By now, most ISVs have
already committed to developing for proprietary platforms, just to stay in
the game.  Where DME might once have been a benefit to them, it now
represents a liability, since it would require them to re-engineer their
products to become DME-compliant.  Meanwhile, vendors have been running
massive PR campaigns to get the press to help them persuade users that it
is safe to choose their proprietary technology. 

	DME proponents would have us believe that the proprietary
platforms and applications users are buying today represent "interim"
solutions which will be replaced or retrofitted to comply with DME.  But
they are not.  To the contrary, they represent the vendors' all-out effort
to establish their platform and application suite as a de facto standard. 
Enough functionality has already been added to these products that, even
with DME, they still would not interoperate without significant tinkering. 

	Though users will suffer from DME's demise, they also bear some of
the responsibility for it.  Only strong, constant pressure from users
could have forced the vendors to follow through on their promises to
develop open standards.  Unfortunately, users were unwilling--indeed,
unable--to wait.  Management tools represent the gating factor to taking
advantage of the economies to be gained from distributed systems.  Users
could not afford to wait for DME's technology to become available--they
needed solutions immediately.  The users got their products, but it was a
Faustian bargain.  The markets will remain proprietary for the foreseeable
future; and users will ultimately pay the price. 

				*	*	*

	Can COSE's systems management succeed for users where OSF failed? 
Don't bet on it. 

	COSE, the Common Operating System Environment, is an effort to
present a unified UNIX front.  It was formed in response to a perceived
threat from Microsoft's Windows NT, just as OSF was formed out of fear of
Sun and AT&T.  COSE may pick up where OSF leaves off in terms of seeking
to develop standard systems management functionality.  However, several
COSE members have already established successful product lines for systems
management.  It's difficult to believe that they would work to undermine
these products by developing a standard providing anything but the most
basic level of integration. 

	So for the next year or two, the press will dutifully report on
COSE systems management meetings, will print lists of user requirements
and diagrams of proposed frameworks, and will avidly describe the
political maneuverings of the members.  Yet at the end of this period, the
proprietary solutions will-- remarkably enough--continue to dominate. 
Users will remain locked in. 

	In future issues, BPR will address the question of how and to what
extent standards should be a part of your management strategy.  In the
mean time, we'll continue to help you find the best tools and the best
management practices to give you cost effective management in the
un-standard, un-open world we and the vendors have built for ourselves. 




