After waiting one week from my original posting, I have GOOD NEWS
and BAD NEWS.

First, the GOOD NEWS:
	I got seven respones to the posting.

Now, the BAD NEWS:
	Five of them were "Please let me know what you find out."
:-)

SUMMARY FROM A CUSTOMER:

The application environment was HP/UX with SYBASE as the RDBMS.

Encina is used to develop set of financial applications
(i.e., General Ledger, Accounts Payable, etc.) in the HP environment.
At present these applications are under development with a BETA release
of the set expected in the next several weeks.  Eventually there 
will be an Encina to IBM mainframe connection using Encina's  
Peer-to-Peer Communication (PPC) component, but no mainframe component
is being developed at present.

For the most part the Encina product has been stable.  The biggest
headache was getting HP/UX, SYBASE, and Encina to integrate.  One
example cited was conflicts in library routines in processing 
certain signals.  Transarc did a good job in helping to resolve the
problems.  The main problem with SYBASE integration was the lack
of support for XA transaction support in SYBASE.  This should not
be an issue in SYBASE10.

No experience with RQS because it was not available on the HP/UX
platform until recently, but they expect to use RQS in the future.

SFS, Encina's filesystem, worked well.

If they had to do things over again, they would have more detailed
discussions with HP, Sybase, and Transarc regarding the integration
of the products.

Most of the people that are working on the development came from a
mainframe environment.  They were re-trained for the UNIX/Encina
environment.  One important skill for developers is to be able
to think/design the application in terms of discrete transactions.
Some outside help was brought in, but the bulk of the people used
on the project were current staff.

SUMMARY FROM A PERSON AFFILIATED WITH TRANSARC:

1)	To use Encina I need the DCE toolkit from Transarc on top
	of SunOS.  How stable is the DCE toolkit?  How easy is it 
	to use?

The product is as stable and easy to use as any DCE. In my opinion, this
means that it is more stable than typical Unix software but probably
requires the same level of expertise as typical Unix software. By the
way, I assume you mean the DCE product; the developer's toolkit was an
early release to get people going and was less stable and usable that
the current version.

If you are using Encina, then you will have a much easier time
developing DCE applications than using raw DCE, regardless of platform.

2)	How ready is Encina for "prime time," i.e., mission critical
	application.

"Mission critical" is in the eye of the beholder. Encina has been
deployed in live environments where real, valuable data cannot be lost.
Can I honestly say that there are 10,000 node production systems to look
at? Not that I'm aware of. Would I put a life support system on it?
Probably not yet. I would use it to start migrating applications from
other environments. Like any new technology, I think the key issue in
going mission critical is not the software per say but the people
involved. No matter how well built the DCE system, for example, if your
operators don't know how to back up and restore the security system,
it's not ready for mission criticial systems. With appropriate training,
DCE and Encina software is certainly ready for prime time.

3)	One of the nice features of Encina is the reliable queueing
	system.  Is it reliable?  How have you used it?

I don't know of any lost queue elements yet! Seriously, at least one
Encina-based product uses the queues heavily and continuously. We have
not had any reports of problems, and I'm told that these people did
failures/recoveries during their system test.

4)	What kind of training is needed to use the product?  How long
	should I plan for a ramp up effort?

The full run of courses is ~2 weeks and you should definitely take it
all (except perhaps the server core module, whch is for people building
their own database system). About 6-9 months for a prototype --
depending on the complexity of the application and the scope of the
project. Could be less, could be more. Roll out also depends a lot on
the target environment and external considerations, e.g., failure and
recovery scenarios, potential performance bottlenecks in infrastructure,
etc. We typically work with customers to help map out a rational
deployment strategy for their situation.

5)	How well does the mainframe (CICS) connectivity works?

I'm told second hand that it works like other APPC-based systems. These
strike me personally as difficult to use, but I have no experience
programming in that environment. The good news is that it looks and
feels just like writing CPI-C code on the mainframe. The bad news is
that it looks and feels just like writing CPI-C code on the mainframe.

